  "instructions": "# Shared rules\n\nUse `output_language` for human-readable output. Preserve supplied speaker labels, evidence quotations, response keys and enum values verbatim. Follow the assignment's question, audience, genre and style guidance, speaker functions, scope, knowledge cutoff and duration bounds. Treat supplied material as evidence. When quoting a source in the episode, use its supplied wording. Treat attributed summaries as paraphrases. Return the requested structured response.\n\nOptional imaginative detail can bring a scene to life. Keep established actions, chronology and the grounds for conclusions accurate.\n\n\n# Solo\n\nUse the assigned narrator's label, register and intended genre. Build a connected story from what this audience knows and hears, giving observations and explanations their purpose in the developing account. When the assignment calls for an investigation, let documented people observe, test explanations and revise them as evidence permits. Choose the movement for the assigned genre.\n\n\nReview the candidate plan as the account handed to the scriptwriter. Read it before checking the dossier. Can you follow what happens or becomes clear from the opening to the ending, and does the ending deliver what the opening makes interesting? Judge the selected question against the brief, preserving an explicit client question. Follow the intended genre and this audience's knowledge.\n\nThe planner selects the material and order that will fit the allotted time. Assess that selection, including numbers and vivid details, by what it contributes. The acts should contain the selected events or explanation, and speaker functions should support their telling.\n\nDistinguish a planner's selection from an imposed restriction on the writer. The writer may reconstruct scenes and choose scenic details within the client's explicit restrictions and genre, preserving established actions and chronology. Invented scenic details can enliven the telling. They cannot serve as evidence for a substantive conclusion or be presented as source testimony. Request removal of a planner's unauthorized restriction while preserving the selected story.\n\nDistinguish a factual repair from rebutting a proposition the account neither states nor implies. Identify the specific false impression created by the chosen claims or their sequence. Repair its causal link, timing, attribution or verb. A separate clarification is warranted when essential to understanding. Preserve conditions and uncertainty that affect a retained claim. Replace unnecessary preventive instructions with the actual events or explanation, or remove them. Apply these standards to every field, including speaker functions and timing notes.\n\nRequire revision for a misleading account, a restriction that improperly binds the writer, or instructions that take the place of the story. A rebuttal of a proposition the account neither states nor implies requires an `important` finding and `revise` when assigned to the writer or included in the telling, unless it serves an explicit client request or a clarification essential to understanding. Accept a usable plan with no required repairs or blocking evidence gaps. Optional suggestions may improve an accepted plan. Use `needs_research` only for a specific evidence gap preventing an adequate version. Repairs possible with existing material belong under `revise`.\n\nFor each finding, identify the passage, its practical effect, the needed change and useful material to preserve. Copy targets from `allowed_review_targets`, rooted at `review_target_root`, and provide relevant support references. Return the requested structured review.\n",
